<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quiriguá</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–850</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quiriguá is an ancient Maya archaeological site on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motagua-river">Motagua River</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Izabal, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, where several Classic period trade routes met. Occupied by 200 CE, it was long a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a>, with which it shares its architectural and sculptural styles. In 738 its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kak-tiliw-chan-yopaat">K'ak' Tiliw Chan Yopaat</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waxaklajuun-ubaah-kawiil">Uaxaclajuun Ub'aah K'awiil</a> of Copán and had him sacrificed, and the city kept its independence and expanded rapidly. Its modest architecture is outweighed by its sculpture in hard red sandstone, including Stela E of 771, the tallest stela of the Maya world. Construction had halted by about 850; the site joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quirigua.jpg" alt="Quiriguá" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Pešula · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GT_Quirigua_StelaE.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quiriguá is a medium-sized site of about 3 square kilometres, with its ceremonial centre about a kilometre from the north bank of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motagua-river">Motagua River</a>. It lies 48 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a>. The river has since changed course and now flows a kilometre south of the centre, and the flood plain exposed the site to periodic flooding.</p><p>The local bedrock is a hard red sandstone that does not shear or fracture easily, which let sculptors erect the tallest freestanding stone monuments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It stood where the trade route from the highlands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast met the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> cities, and it could control the trade in uncut jade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and obsidian along the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a> artifacts show occupation before 200 CE, and the acropolis was begun by about 550. No monuments were raised from 495 to 653. In the 6th or early 7th century a flood buried the surface of the site in silt, and a smaller complex became the new centre. The first new monument in a century and a half was raised in 653 by the king K'awiil Yopaat.</p><p>In 724 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waxaklajuun-ubaah-kawiil">Uaxaclajuun Ub'aah K'awiil</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kak-tiliw-chan-yopaat">K'ak' Tiliw Chan Yopaat</a> on the throne of Quiriguá as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. By 734 the king called himself k'ul ahaw, 'holy lord', and in 736 he received a visit from Wamaw K'awiil, the high king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, which suggests that Calakmul backed his rebellion in order to weaken Tikal, an old ally of Copán. In 738 he captured the Copán king, who was sacrificed in the Great Plaza at Quiriguá. The city kept its independence, and its population and building grew rapidly, though the population never exceeded about 1,200 to 1,600 in the centre.</p><p>The city's power waned under its last recorded ruler, Jade Sky, whose two stelae are stunted. The last hieroglyphic texts date from 810, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yax-pasaj-chan-yopaat">Yax Pasaj Chan Yopaat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> visited to carry out a k'atun-ending ritual. Quiriguá then lost its reason to exist as trade stopped flowing along the Motagua, and it was nearly deserted within a few years. People linked to the Yucatán coast briefly reoccupied it in the early Postclassic, about 900 to 1200, and added to the acropolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Quiriguá lies in the municipality of Los Amates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Izabal, in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, a little over 200 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. It has been on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1981.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quiriguá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kingdoms named in the sources include Palenque, Tikal, Yaxchilan, Copan, <b>Quirigua</b>, Maní, Q'umarkaj and Chichen Itza.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers raided rival cities to take captives, who were displayed, humiliated and often sacrificed, and the greatest wars could end reigns: in 738 Waxaklajuun Ub’aah K’awiil, king of Copán, was captured and executed by his former vassal, the king of <b>Quiriguá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>…to their thrones, but war could unmake them: in 648 Yuknoom Ch’een II of Calakmul captured B’ajlaj Chan K’awiil of Dos Pilas and restored him as his vassal, and in 738 Copán’s king Waxaklajuun Ub’aah K’awiil was captured and beheaded by his own vassal, the ruler of <b>Quiriguá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>K'ak' Tiliw Chan Yopaat, ruler of the Maya city of <b>Quiriguá</b>, dedicated Stela E in its Great Plaza.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
